Yes. We already had the stopping rule.

The answer wasn’t “compound forever.” It was:

keep reinvesting while marginal validated capability + learning + option value > added complexity + cost + lock-in + downstream risk

Then harvest when that flips.

So the loop was:

build
→ compound
→ validate
→ metabolize into reusable capability
→ watch diminishing returns
→ harvest/use
→ reset
→ reinvest selectively

Two older phrases we had fit it cleanly:

“Compound before expending.”
and then later: “Build roads behind the horse.”

Meaning: don’t just accumulate potential forever. Convert successful iterations into infrastructure, doctrine, tools, skills, reputation, money, freedom, or whatever the actual payoff is.

The explicit threshold we had was essentially:

Stop recursive expansion when the marginal validated capability or compression gain falls below the cost of added complexity.

That’s the switch.

So in compact form:

reinvest while the slope is good
harvest when the slope flattens
keep a reserve for optionality
re-enter compounding when a new high-leverage branch appears

And that is probably the thing you were reaching for: you don’t choose between compounding and using. You oscillate between them according to marginal return.